  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>CQC assesses adult social care in England through the single assessment framework and five key questions: safe, effective, caring, responsive and well-led</li><li>Each key question breaks into quality statements, and CQC gathers evidence against them</li><li>The Health and Social Care Act 2008 (Regulated Activities) Regulations 2014 set the fundamental standards a service must meet</li><li>A mock audit finds gaps early and builds evidence; it does not make the home compliant, the registered manager acting on it does</li><li>Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland use different regulators and frameworks, so build the audit around the one that applies</li></ul></div><h2>What a CQC compliance audit checklist is for</h2><p>A mock CQC audit walks a care home against the framework the inspector uses, so the service finds and fixes its gaps before the real inspection. It also builds the evidence file that a good judgement rests on: photos of the environment, observations against each quality statement, the state of policies and records at the point of check.</p><p>A mock audit does not make the home compliant. The registered manager acting on it does. What the audit does is turn a vague worry about the next inspection into a specific, evidenced list of things to fix, with an owner and a date against each one.</p><h2>The single assessment framework and five key questions</h2><p>CQC assesses adult social care in England using the single assessment framework, built on five key questions: is the service safe, effective, caring, responsive and well-led. Under each key question sit quality statements, written as \"we\" statements describing what good looks like. CQC gathers evidence against those statements and reaches a rating. A mock audit mirrors this structure so its findings map to what an inspector assesses.</p><h3>Safe and effective</h3><p>Is the home safe from avoidable harm, with working safeguarding, risk management and safe care and treatment? Does the care achieve good outcomes for people?</p><h3>Caring and responsive</h3><p>Are people treated with kindness and dignity, involved in decisions, and does the service respond to individual needs, choices and complaints?</p><h3>Well-led</h3><p>Is there clear, accountable leadership, an open and honest culture, and governance that actually works? Inspections often turn on this key question.</p><h2>The fundamental standards</h2><p>Underneath the framework sit the fundamental standards, set by the Health and Social Care Act 2008 (Regulated Activities) Regulations 2014: person-centred care, dignity and respect, consent, safe care and treatment, safeguarding, good governance, staffing and fit and proper persons, among others. A mock audit tests the home against these as well as the quality statements. The registered manager is the named duty holder and is accountable for acting on what the audit finds.</p><h2>What to look for in a mock CQC audit tool</h2><ul><li><strong>Maps to the framework:</strong> findings recorded against the current key questions and quality statements, not a generic checklist</li><li><strong>Evidence at the point of observation:</strong> photos and notes captured on the walk, tied to the statement they evidence</li><li><strong>Action plan out of the box:</strong> each gap becomes an action with an owner and a date, not a filed document</li><li><strong>A dashboard across homes:</strong> a regional lead sees every home's mock-audit position without chasing spreadsheets</li><li><strong>Right scope:</strong> the tool audits evidence and process; it is not a care record and no resident data is entered</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the mock walk and the evidence and turns it into an action plan. It does not make the home compliant, and it is not a care management system or clinical record. Work from CQC's current published framework, as it is updated, and treat this page as general information, not regulatory advice. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your firm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is a CQC compliance audit checklist?</h3><p>It is a structured walk-through that tests a care service against CQC's single assessment framework, its five key questions (safe, effective, caring, responsive, well-led) and the quality statements under them, plus the fundamental standards in the 2014 Regulations. It is run internally, as a mock inspection, to find and fix gaps before CQC arrives.</p><h3>Does a mock CQC audit have to follow a set format?</h3><p>There is no single statutory format for an internal audit. The value comes from mirroring the current published framework and quality statements so your findings map to what an inspector assesses. Always work from CQC's current guidance, as it is updated, and adapt the checklist to your service. Treat this as general information, not regulatory advice.</p><h3>Does this apply outside England?</h3><p>CQC regulates in England only. Scotland uses the Care Inspectorate, Wales uses Care Inspectorate Wales and Northern Ireland uses RQIA, each with its own quality framework. Quickler lets you build the mock audit around whichever framework applies rather than hard-coding CQC's. Check your regulator's current guidance.</p><h3>Can I run a mock CQC inspection over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The quality lead receives each prompt in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note or a photo against each quality statement, and the report and action plan generate automatically. No separate app or login is required, and quickler is not a clinical record, so no resident care data is entered.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/care-home-audit-software-uk.html\">Care home audit software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/care-home-health-safety-audit-uk.html\">Care home health and safety audit</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/ipc-audit-care-home-uk.html\">IPC audit for care homes</a></li></ul>",
